# r02 BUG (silent wrong behaviour; a spec claim that does not hold) - SPEC T2b: reading through a null reference is
# undefined behaviour, "in practice a deterministic trap, address zero being unmapped". Under the default build
# (-O3 + LTO) LLVM inlines Peek, proves the field null, and deletes everything from the read on - including the
# function's return - so control falls off the end of the program's main into the next function in the binary, the
# C main that calls it: the program prints its first two lines again and again, forever. Peeking an empty stack is
# the commonest null bug there is.
#   objdump: null5_main ends "call std_prelude_print_println; nopw" with no ret, and <main> follows.
# Variants in ../min: null4.olang (the empty peek first) prints nothing and exits with status 48; null1.olang
# ("p P& = null; println($p.x)") prints 20 garbage bytes and exits 0; null3.olang prints an earlier line twice.
# Where the null comes through a search the compiler cannot see through (min/null2.olang), the trap happens.
# -d traps (status 139) in every variant; -i stops with "it reads through a null reference".
#
# expected: start / after a push: 4 / then a trap (status 139), as -d gives - and as -b gives with LLVM's
#           null_pointer_is_valid attribute on the module's functions (tried by hand on this IR: 139)
# actual  -b: "start" and "after a push: 4" repeated without end (9,091 times in 2 seconds), never a trap
type Node struct(v I64, next Node&) {
    v
    next
}

type Stack struct() {
    top Node& = null
    n I64 = 0
}

fn (s mut Stack&) Push(v I64) {
    s.top = Node(v, s.top)
    s.n++
}

fn (s Stack&) Peek() I64 { return s.top.v }

fn main() ? {
    println("start")
    s := Stack()
    s.Push(4)
    println("after a push: " $s.Peek())
    t := Stack()
    println("peek of an empty stack: " $t.Peek())
    println("end")
}
